Chapter: PciBusMaster
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 The predecessor set the memory-space bit alone. That is enough for a
 device whose registers you poke and nothing else, and it is not enough
 for any device that moves data on its own initiative: a descriptor-ring
 NIC fetches descriptors and DMAs frame bodies itself, and with bus
 master clear it is addressable, apparently configured, and silent.
 Nothing in the tree would have reported that, because nothing called
 the function.

 THE BEFORE LEG IS THE CONTROL. codex-vm brings every PCI device up
 with command 0x0003 -- I/O and memory set, bus master CLEAR -- so
 "after = 7" only means something beside "before = 3". Were the device
 already mastering, this test would pass while proving nothing, which is
 the shape of a function that always answers the same thing.

Section: Command Register

  cmd-of : Integer -> Integer
  cmd-of (dev) = bit-and (pci-read-config 0 dev 0 4) 65535

  has-bus-master : Integer -> Text
  has-bus-master (cmd) = if bit-and cmd 4 > 0 then "T" else "F"

Section: Probe

  PciProbe = record {
    p-before : Integer,
    p-after : Integer,
    p-other : Integer
  }

  probe : Integer -> PciProbe
  probe (other) =
    let before = cmd-of 0
    in let d = PciDevice { pci-bus = 0, pci-dev = 0, pci-func = 0, pci-vendor = 0, pci-device-id = 0, pci-class = 0, pci-subclass = 0, pci-header-type = 0 }
    in let w = pci-enable-device d
    in let after = cmd-of 0
    in let untouched = cmd-of other
    in PciProbe { p-before = before, p-after = after, p-other = untouched }

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let r = probe 1
    in act
      print-line-uni ("dev0-before=" & show (r.p-before))
      print-line-uni ("dev0-after=" & show (r.p-after))
      print-line-uni ("bus-master-before=" & has-bus-master (r.p-before))
      print-line-uni ("bus-master-after=" & has-bus-master (r.p-after))
      print-line-uni ("dev1-untouched=" & show (r.p-other))
    end
  end

